<TopicPage slug="subjects/peace-conflict-studies/foundational/what-are-peace-and-violence">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Peace and conflict studies opens with definitional surgery on two words everyone thinks
    they already understand — and the surgery matters because the definitions decide the
    field's entire agenda. Is a country "at peace" the moment its civil war's shooting stops,
    even if its cities remain segregated, its courts corrupt and its mortality rates show
    who gets to live? Is "violence" only what a person does to another person, or can an
    arrangement — a policy, a border, an economic structure — be violent without anyone
    pulling a trigger? How you answer determines what you study, what you fund and what
    you count as success.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field's analytical spine comes from Johan Galtung, the Norwegian sociologist who
    founded the first peace research institute and journal. His framework — negative versus
    positive peace, and violence as direct, structural or cultural — radicalised the
    discipline with one move: <em>a country without gunfire can still be violent, if its
    arrangements quietly shorten lives</em>. That single claim redefined peacebuilding as
    something far larger than ceasefire mediation, and it remains the best entry point into
    the field — partly because it is controversial, and the controversy teaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page works through the three foundation topics: the negative–positive peace
    distinction, the three-part violence typology, and the empirical turn — how a field
    built on contested concepts measures itself with datasets and indices. Along the way it
    takes the framework's critics seriously, because knowing where a definition breaks is
    part of knowing it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Galtung's pyramid: peace, direct, structural, cultural" widget="equationStep" config={galtungCfg} />

  <h2>Negative and positive peace</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Negative peace</strong> is the absence of direct violence: the shooting stops.
    It is what most people mean by "peace" and what most diplomacy delivers — ceasefires,
    armistices, the end of open hostilities. It is genuinely valuable, and it is
    insufficient, because a ceasefire can freeze a conflict as easily as end it: the guns
    silence while the grievances, the displaced populations and the armed actors all
    remain, waiting. Korea's armistice has "held" since 1953; nobody calls the peninsula
    peace. Northern Ireland's Troubles paused and restarted repeatedly before 1998, and the
    Good Friday Agreement's durability came not from stopping the shooting but from
    rebuilding the conditions — power-sharing, decommissioning, cross-border institutions —
    that made resumption unlikely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Positive peace</strong> is the presence of the conditions under which violence
    is unlikely: functioning and fair institutions, equitable access to opportunity,
    freedom of information, integration across social divides. Galtung's formulation — the
    absence of <em>structural</em> violence — makes it the mirror concept of the violence
    typology below. The distinction explains a pattern the field finds constantly: wars
    relapse. A substantial share of countries emerging from civil war fall back into it
    within a decade, and the relapse risk concentrates where the negative peace was never
    followed by positive peace — disarmament without reintegration, elections without
    institutions, ceasefires without grievance settlement. The definitions are not academic;
    they decide what gets funded and measured. If peace is merely the absence of shooting,
    a ceasefire counts as success and its collapse is mysterious. If peace requires positive
    conditions, the ceasefire is only the beginning and the field's job is to identify,
    build and measure those conditions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> positive peace is just a nicer word for stability. It is
    nearly the opposite — stability can be imposed by repression (a violent "peace"), while
    positive peace is specified by justice and functioning institutions. Authoritarian
    order scores as negative peace with structural violence intact; the typology is built
    to keep the two from being confused.
  </p>

  <h2>Direct, structural and cultural violence</h2>
  <p>
    Galtung's violence typology has three layers, best understood as a pyramid whose base
    legitimises its top. <strong>Direct violence</strong> is the visible layer: an
    identifiable actor doing identifiable harm — war, assault, murder, torture. It is what
    news covers, what law punishes, and what most people mean by violence. It is also, in
    scale, the smallest layer: epidemiologists who try to count lives lost by cause find
    that preventable death from arrangements — absent clinics, contaminated water, famine
    amid plenty — dwarfs death by weapons.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Structural violence</strong> is harm built into arrangements, with no actor and
    no moment of harm — and Galtung's example (1969) remains the clearest: two people die of
    the same treatable pneumonia; one in a country with clinics, one without. No one pulled
    a trigger, but the arrangement shortened a life, and it would have shortened a
    statistically predictable number of lives — which is what makes the concept analytical
    rather than rhetorical. Epidemiology now quantifies this as excess mortality; the field
    reads it as violence-by-structure and asks the framework's sharpest question: who
    benefits from the arrangement persisting? The concept's power is that it keeps visible
    what comfortable societies prefer to dissolve into background statistics — the
    different life expectancies across neighbourhoods of the same city are, on this
    analysis, a peace-and-conflict fact, not just a public-health one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cultural violence</strong> (Galtung's 1990 addition) is the legitimating layer:
    the stories, symbols, religions, ideologies and media frames that make the other two
    forms look normal, natural or deserved. Dehumanising propaganda precedes genocides;
    "they are not really like us" narratives soften discrimination into policy; honour
    codes and caste logic teach that certain harms are proper. Cultural violence is also
    where the framework is most contested — critics note that marking a culture "violent"
    risks its own kind of prejudice, and that the category can blur into disapproval of
    beliefs rather than analysis of harm. The careful version is the honest one: cultural
    violence names <em>specific stories used to legitimate specific harms</em>, not cultures
    as wholes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> calling structures "violent" is mere metaphor. The
    framework's defence is criterion-based: violence means avoidable harm that shortens or
    damages life. If that harm is inflicted by an actor, it is direct; if by an arrangement
    that could be otherwise, it is structural. Whether the extension of the word is wise is
    a fair debate — but the definition is doing analytical work, not decoration.
  </p>

  <h2>Conflict analysis: the iceberg and the triangle</h2>
  <p>
    The definitional work feeds the field's first practical tool: conflict analysis. The
    classic <strong>iceberg diagram</strong> teaches that a conflict's visible behaviour —
    the fighting, the protest, the breakdown — floats on layers beneath it: beneath the
    behaviour sit the <strong>attitudes</strong> (fear, grievance, dehumanising narratives —
    cultural violence's territory), and beneath the attitudes sit the
    <strong>contradictions</strong>: the structural incompatibilities (contested land,
    excluded groups, economic imbalance) that generate the conflict in the first place.
    Galtung's own <strong>conflict triangle</strong> names the same structure —
    behaviour, attitude, contradiction — and its warning is operational: resolve only the
    behaviour (a ceasefire) while attitudes and contradictions persist, and the conflict
    regenerates; the field's term for this is latent conflict, the iceberg underwater,
    waiting. Positive peace, on this analysis, is simply a society whose triangle is
    managed — whose contradictions are addressable through institutions rather than
    violence. The iceberg is why the field distrusts quick wins: any peace process that
    never goes below the waterline is a schedule, not a settlement.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring peace</h2>
  <p>
    Modern peace research quantifies what it studies — precisely because "peace" is
    contested and measurement disciplines the definitions. The <strong>Global Peace
    Index</strong> (Institute for Economics and Peace, published annually since 2007) scores
    about 160 countries on 23 indicators across three domains: the level of societal safety
    and security, the extent of ongoing domestic and international conflict, and the degree
    of militarisation — mixing hard data (battle deaths, refugees, homicides, imprisoned
    populations) with qualitative expert assessments. Its companion <strong>Positive Peace
    Index</strong> measures the institutional conditions — well-functioning government, low
    corruption, equitable distribution, good business environment, free information flow,
    high human capital, acceptance of others' rights — operationalising Galtung's positive
    peace into eight pillars that the GPI's own analysis links to resilience and relapse
    risk.
  </p>
  <p>
    The academic datasets run deeper and longer. The <strong>Uppsala Conflict Data
    Program</strong> (UCDP) has coded armed conflicts since 1946 with strict thresholds
    (a "war" requires 1,000 battle-related deaths in a year; smaller conflicts are coded
    separately), making cross-country comparison and time-series analysis possible; the
    <strong>Correlates of War</strong> project has coded wars since 1816; casualty archives
    and event datasets track violence at fine grain. This is the field's empirical turn:
    the numbers do not settle the definitional debates — but they expose patterns the
    definitions must reckon with, such as the post-1945 decline of interstate war relative
    to civil conflict, or the concentration of conflict relapse in low-positive-peace
    societies.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The framework's critics sharpen it.</strong> Three objections recur. First,
    <em>expansion</em>: if violence includes structures, the concept risks covering
    everything and explaining nothing — the reply is the avoidability criterion, which
    keeps the concept bounded even if bounded debates remain. Second, <em>agency</em>:
    structural violence names no perpetrator, which troubles both law (who is tried?) and
    politics (who is responsible?) — the framework answers with the benefits question
    rather than a culprit, which is coherent but unsatisfying in courtrooms. Third,
    <em>comparability</em>: how much structural violence equals how much direct violence?
    There is no exchange rate; the typology clarifies categories, not trade-offs. Peace
    researchers who hold all three objections in view use the framework as a lens for
    generating hypotheses and questions, not a formula for verdicts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The definitions have already shaped practice.</strong> Peacebuilding
    architecture — the UN's peacebuilding commission and fund, the World Bank's
    conflict-and-development work, the humanitarian–development–peace "triple nexus" —
    operationalises positive peace: after a ceasefire, the intervention portfolio includes
    institution-building, reconciliation processes, economic inclusion and security-sector
    reform, because the relapse data says those are the levers. Post-conflict economics
    (Paul Collier's "conflict trap" analysis) and reconciliation practice (truth
    commissions, which treat cultural violence directly by re-authorising the stories a
    society tells) are the framework's children. Later units apply this rigour to the
    causes of war, the methods of peacebuilding, and the justice that follows atrocities —
    but every one of those units begins here, with a definition of peace that is wider than
    the absence of gunfire, and a definition of violence that asks what the arrangement is
    doing to the life expectancy of the least powerful person inside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Conflict sensitivity closes the introductory loop.</strong> Once peace and
    violence have been redefined this broadly, an uncomfortable implication follows for
    everyone who acts in conflict zones — aid agencies, companies, peacekeepers: every
    intervention enters the conflict system and changes it. The field's answer is
    <strong>conflict sensitivity</strong> (crystallised in the "Do No Harm" framework):
    analyse the dividers and connectors before acting — who is being empowered, whose
    grievance is being fed, which local rivalry your resource distribution is about to
    fund — so that well-intentioned programmes stop accidentally doing harm. The classic
    warning cases are structural in Galtung's exact sense: relief routed through one
    ethnic group's networks, a reconstruction contract that employed one side's militias,
    a new well that redrew whose land was worth fighting for. Positive peace, applied,
    means designing interventions that connect rather than divide — and that habit of
    second-order thinking is the first professional skill this definitional work exists
    to produce.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galtung, "Violence, Peace, and Peace Research" (1969); Barash &amp; Webel, <em>Peace and Conflict Studies</em>; Global Peace Index methodology.</p>
